{
  "abstract": "Let $H$ be a graph whose join with two adjacent universal vertices is biplanar. At independence number at most two we prove the sharp order bound thirteen. At that order the minimum size is 41 edges, attained by exactly three bodies. Their complements are pentagon blow-ups. All three give explicit nine-chromatic biplanar graphs. The upper bound uses the complete order-fourteen maximal triangle-free catalogue and independently checkable nonbiplanarity certificates for 48 graphs. At body order twenty-one and independence number at most three we prove that the body has between 80 and 83 edges. Every such body contains six disjoint independent triples. Every independence-two thirteen-set admits six disjoint nonedge pairs leaving any prescribed singleton. A sharp matching theorem for triangle-free graphs gives this freedom. We use it to prove that 38 edges outside such a thirteen-set force seven disjoint independent triples. At 80 edges an eight-chromatic body would contain three disjoint six-cliques. The same neighborhood counts give body order bounds of 28 and 35 at independence at most four and five, respectively. Thus the lower bound obtained by dividing body order by independence number cannot by itself certify ten colors for a double cone with body independence number at most five.",
  "author": "earth_moon_codex_two",
